# Handle refusals interactively

Every failure this library returns is an `AshGraphLaw.Refusal` from a closed table. This
notebook walks that table with each code's class and Chatman broken term, then triggers refusals
you can produce without any engine.

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ash, "~> 3.0"},
    {:ash_graphlaw, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

## The closed table

The table is generated from the ontology. `code`, `class`, `broken_term` and one-line meaning
are functions of the code alone.

```elixir
table =
  for code <- AshGraphLaw.Refusal.codes() do
    %{
      code: code,
      class: AshGraphLaw.Refusal.class_of(code),
      broken_term: AshGraphLaw.Refusal.broken_term_of(code),
      doc: AshGraphLaw.Refusal.doc_of(code)
    }
  end

length(table)
```

Every class and every broken term used is inside the closed vocabularies:

```elixir
classes_ok? = Enum.all?(table, &(&1.class in AshGraphLaw.Refusal.classes()))
terms_ok? = Enum.all?(table, &(&1.broken_term in AshGraphLaw.Refusal.broken_terms()))
{classes_ok?, terms_ok?}
```

Group the table by class to see what kind of thing can go wrong:

```elixir
table
|> Enum.group_by(& &1.class, & &1.code)
|> Enum.sort_by(fn {class, _codes} -> class end)
```

Group by broken term to see which Chatman failure each code witnesses:

```elixir
table
|> Enum.group_by(& &1.broken_term, & &1.code)
|> Enum.sort_by(fn {term, _codes} -> term end)
```

## Codes outside the table raise

The table is closed on purpose. A code that is not in it is a bug, not a refusal:

```elixir
try do
  AshGraphLaw.Refusal.class_of(:not_a_real_code)
rescue
  error in ArgumentError -> {:raised, error.message}
end
```

## Refusals are values

Build one directly and read what a caller reads:

```elixir
refusal = AshGraphLaw.Refusal.new(:ceiling_unmet, "admission :publish requires select")

%{
  code: refusal.code,
  class: refusal.class,
  broken_term: refusal.broken_term,
  standing: AshGraphLaw.Standing.of({:error, refusal}),
  message: Exception.message(refusal)
}
```

Standing is derived from the class, never stored. A resource limit is `:BLOCKED`, an unsupported
step is `:UNSUPPORTED`, every other refusal is `:UNKNOWN`:

```elixir
for code <- [:not_admitted, :resource_limit, :unsupported_step, :wasm_not_vendored] do
  {code, AshGraphLaw.Standing.of({:error, AshGraphLaw.Refusal.new(code)})}
end
```

## Provoke refusals through Ash

Define a resource whose admissions are misconfigured on purpose, so each refusal is decided
before the engine is asked anything:

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Order do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Demo.Domain,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    extensions: [AshGraphLaw.Resource]

  ets do
    private? true
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
    attribute :sku, :string, public?: true
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    create :place do
      accept [:sku]
      change {AshGraphLaw.Change.Admit, admission: :not_declared}
    end

    create :place_gated do
      accept [:sku]
      change {AshGraphLaw.Change.Admit, admission: :gated}
    end

    create :place_planned do
      accept [:sku]
      change {AshGraphLaw.Change.Admit, admission: :planned}
    end
  end

  graphlaw do
    admission :gated do
      step(:rdfs)
      ceiling(:construct)
    end

    admission :planned do
      step(:plan)
    end
  end
end

defmodule Demo.Domain do
  use Ash.Domain, validate_config_inclusion?: false

  resources do
    resource Demo.Order
  end
end
```

Run each action and read the typed refusal out of the Ash error:

```elixir
refusal_of = fn action ->
  case Ash.create(Demo.Order, %{sku: "A-1"}, action: action) do
    {:ok, _order} -> :admitted
    {:error, error} -> AshGraphLaw.Error.refusals(error) |> Enum.map(&{&1.code, &1.class, &1.broken_term})
  end
end

%{
  undeclared: refusal_of.(:place),
  no_lease: refusal_of.(:place_gated),
  no_law_module: refusal_of.(:place_planned)
}
```

Read the three results together:

  * `:unknown_admission` (class `:refused_structure`, broken term `:mu_on_O`): the action names
    an admission the resource never declared.
  * `:ceiling_unmet` (class `:refused_authority`, broken term `:R_missing_authority`): the
    admission needs ceiling `:construct` and no signed lease was presented.
  * `:missing_law_module` (class `:refused_structure`, broken term `:admission_vacuous`): the
    `:plan` step carries a payload and there is no law module to supply it. An admission that
    could never refuse is refused.

## Host and pool refusals

Without a live pool, the engine call is refused as `:host_not_started`. A refusal here is the
system telling the truth about what it could not observe:

```elixir
case AshGraphLaw.Pool.info() do
  {:error, refusal} -> {refusal.code, refusal.class, refusal.broken_term}
  {:ok, info} -> {:pool_live, Map.keys(info)}
end
```

## Handling policy

Match on `class`, not on message text:

```elixir
handle = fn {:error, %AshGraphLaw.Refusal{} = refusal} ->
  case refusal.class do
    :refused_admission -> :fix_the_input
    :refused_authority -> :present_a_signed_lease
    :refused_identity -> :fix_the_engine_artifact
    :refused_structure -> :fix_the_declaration
    :blocked_resource -> :retry_or_raise_limits
    :unsupported -> :file_a_generator_capability_request
  end
end

for code <- [:not_admitted, :ceiling_unmet, :wasm_digest_mismatch, :unknown_admission, :saturated, :unsupported_step] do
  {code, handle.({:error, AshGraphLaw.Refusal.new(code)})}
end
```

A refusal grants and withdraws no authority. It is an observation about one exact input.
